One of the most exciting use of AI code assistant is to help you write new code from scratch or integrate a new feature into an existin g codebase.
The challenge here is that this task are usually open-ended they are various ways of implementing a feature.
This guide is about guiding the AI to produce code that fits your needs and style.
Below are the strategies to do that:

    Starting with high-level instructions, then drill down 
        Begin outlining what you want to build in plain language, possibly breaking it into smaller tasks.
        For example 
            Outline a plan to add a search feature that filters a list of products by name in React app. The products are fetched from an API 
        By dividing each feature, you ensure each prompt is specific and the response are managable.
        This also mirrors iterative development - you can test piece as it's built.

    Provide relevant context or reference code 
        If you are adding a feature to an existing project, it helps tremendously to show the AI how similar things are done in that project.
        For example 
            Here is an existing UserList component (code..).Now create a ProductList component that is similar but includes a search bar.
        Having relevant files open or referencing them in your prompt provides contaxt that leads to more project-specific and consistent code suggestions.
        Another trick is when your project uses a particular coding style or archiitecture mention that 
        For example 
            We use Redux for state managment-integrate the search state into Redux store.
        
    Use comments and TODOs as inline prompts
        When working directly in an IDE with copilot, one effective workflow is writting a comment that describes the next chunk of code you need, then letting the AI autocomplete it.
        For example 
            In a Node.js backend, you might write 
            //TODO: Validate the request payload (ensure name and email are provided) and then start the next line.
        Copilot often picks up on the intent and generate a block of code performing the validation.
        However, be perpared to edit the generated code if the AI misinterprets as always, verify its correctness.

    Provide examples of expected input/output or usage 
        For example 
            Implement a function formatPrice (amount) in JavaScript that takes a number (like 2.5) and returns a string formatted in USD (like $2.50) should return '$2.50'

    When the result isn't what you want, rewrite the prompt with more detail or constraints
        It's common for first generation of code for a new feature doesn't nail it.
        Treat the AI as a junior dev who gave a first draft - now you need to give a feedback.
        For example 
            The solution works but i'd prefer if you used the built-in array filter method instead of a for loop
            or 
            can you refactor the generated component to use React Hooks for state instead of a class component? Our codebase is all functional components.
        Figure out what you are lacking and clarify it.

    
